				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/veiedhimaedhr" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3737554); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=3737554&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607373848" alt="Veiedhimaedhr" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=3737554)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/veiedhimaedhr" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3737554); return false;" >Veiedhimaedhr</a></span> <span class="odate time_1521410184 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">18 Mar 2018 21:56</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3743028">
									

<p>So if you've read the article, human experimentation is carried out 577 times before being halted. It's been brought up as a point of disbelief here, and it also survived the feedback sweeps during drafting so I thought I'd open it up here for discussion.</p>
<p>The idea was to make a mini-zoo(San Diego Zoo has 3700 individual animals and London Zoo has 16000) that was both reasonable to the Foundation's rapid consumption of D-Class personnel and supportive of the 'Scarcliffe's manic search for a reversal' narrative. However, I don't want to sacrifice disbelief and finding a sweet middle ground would be ideal if I can find one.</p>
<p>So what do you guys think? Should the number stay, or is there another number that you think would achieve both goals?</p>

							</div>

								
